      body: "A root canal treats the soft pulp inside a tooth when it is infected or badly inflamed. The aim is to keep your own tooth instead of removing it.\n\nFirst, an X-ray shows how far the infection has spread and how many canals the tooth has. The area is then numbed with a local anaesthetic, and a thin rubber sheet keeps the tooth dry.\n\nThe dentist makes a small opening, cleans the canals with fine files and rinses them. They are then filled and sealed. Most teeth need one or two visits of about an hour.\n\nFor a day or two the tooth can feel tender when you bite. A back tooth usually needs a crown afterwards, because a treated tooth is more brittle and can crack.\n\nAsk to see your X-ray before and after. It shows you exactly what was done.",
