      excerpt: "Blood in the sink is not normal, and it is not a reason to brush less. Usually it is early gum disease, and it can be reversed.",
      body: "Healthy gums do not bleed when you brush. Bleeding is most often gingivitis, an early gum inflammation caused by plaque left along the gum line.\n\nThe common reaction is to brush more gently or avoid the area. That lets more plaque build up. Keep brushing twice a day with a soft brush, angled towards the gum, and clean between teeth once a day.\n\nIf bleeding does not settle in two weeks, see a dentist. Hardened plaque (tartar) cannot be brushed off and needs scaling. Left alone, gingivitis can progress to periodontitis, where the bone around the teeth is lost and teeth can loosen.\n\nSome things raise the risk: diabetes, smoking and gutka, pregnancy, and some medicines. Tell your dentist about them. Bleeding with bruising elsewhere or unexplained tiredness needs a doctor's check as well.",
